  "title": "Spike-Timing-Dependent Plasticity",
  "emoji": "🔄",
  "color": "#7E22CE",
  "description": "A tiny lesson with a big payoff: Spike-Timing-Dependent Plasticity.",
  "is_free": false,
  "published": true,
  "story": {
    "hook": {
      "text": "The order in which two neurons fire matters more than whether they fire at the same time.",
      "visual": "⏱️"
    },
    "buildup": {
      "text": "If neuron A fires just before neuron B, the synapse from A to B strengthens. The timing must be within about 20 milliseconds.",
      "visual": "➡️"
    },
    "discovery": {
      "text": "If neuron B fires before neuron A, the synapse weakens. The brain interprets this as A not causing B.",
      "visual": "⬅️"
    },
    "twist": {
      "text": "This millisecond-precise rule lets the brain learn cause and effect. It is how you learn that pressing a key makes a sound.",
      "visual": "🎹"
    },
    "climax": {
      "text": "Spike-timing-dependent plasticity, or STDP, turns Hebb's rule into a precise timing mechanism for the real world.",
      "visual": "🎯"
    },
    "punchline": {
      "text": "The brain does not just notice what happens together. It notices what happens first.",
      "visual": "🥇"
    }
  },
  "quiz": {
    "question": "What happens if neuron A fires just before neuron B?",
    "options": [
      "The synapse from A to B strengthens",
      "The synapse from A to B weakens",
      "Both neurons are silenced"
    ],
    "correct": 0
  },
